            'Septoria tritici blotch, caused by Zymoseptoria tritici, links autumn–winter sources, weather-dependent establishment and later disease on the upper canopy. Local residue, infected living leaves and imported inoculum can contribute to the seasonal sequence. Leaf appearance changes the receiving tissue available to infection, while canopy architecture affects subsequent contact and splash transfer. Temperature influences crop development and disease progression; humidity and precipitation influence the effective exposure supporting establishment. The resulting damage cannot be interpreted from a terminal severity measurement alone. [[SuffertSache2011|ShawRoyle1989|LovellCanopy2004|Baccar2011|Lovell2004]]',
